SUMMARY:The Free Exercise of Religion in History\, Tradition\, and the Co
 nstitution
DESCRIPTION:Challenging widely accepted accounts of First Amendment histo
 ry\, newly discovered historical documents reveal that the First Amendme
 nt’s Free Exercise Clause was meant to protect only the freedom to wor
 ship\, not broader rights of conscience. That meaning persisted until th
 e middle of the 20th century when Justice William Brennan\, the father o
 f the living Constitution\, dramatically expanded Free Exercise rights. 
 Originalism icon Justice Antonin Scalia narrowed them three decades ago.
  Now\, most of the justices have said they want to re-expand Free Exerci
 se rights\, but they have also been committ\nhttps://www.princeton.edu/e
 vents/2026/free-exercise-religion-history-tradition-and-constitution
URL:https://www.princeton.edu/events/2026/free-exercise-religion-history-
 tradition-and-constitution
STATUS:CONFIRMED
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
END:VCALENDAR
